const makeQuestions=items=>items.map(([q,options,answer,why,lesson])=>({q,options,answer,why,lesson}));

export const lessons = [
 {title:'A shared purpose, clear responsibilities',time:'6 min',intro:'Good teamwork starts when people understand the purpose of the activity and the limits of their own role.',body:[
  'Role clarity is a simple idea with a powerful effect: people work better when they know what they are responsible for, what they are not responsible for, who can change the plan and where to get help. When roles are vague, helpful people often fill gaps quietly. That can feel cooperative in the moment, but it can also create hidden risk because nobody can see which duty has been left uncovered.',
  'Psychologists often describe this as reducing cognitive load. If a volunteer has to guess the task, remember changing instructions, manage visitors and decide who has authority all at once, their attention is split. A clear role frees attention for the human part of the work: noticing who needs support, communicating warmly and spotting concerns early.',
  'Before an activity begins, ask for the practical details: where you should be, when your duty starts and ends, what decisions you can make, what information you may share and who coordinates changes. A useful role description also says what is outside your role. Being willing to help does not mean being available for every task.',
  'The roles in this training are examples for learning. Each organisation must identify its own activity lead, safeguarding route and escalation arrangements. If nobody can clarify a responsibility, explain the gap and ask for an agreed plan before taking on work you cannot safely do.'
 ],example:'“I can welcome people at the entrance until 8 pm. Who will cover me for a break, and who should I ask if the plan changes?”',reflect:'Where have you seen a task become harder because nobody had named the owner, cover or decision route?'},
 {title:'Authority gradients and respectful challenge',time:'6 min',intro:'Respect and challenge can sit together. A person can listen carefully to a leader while still checking a request that seems unsafe or unclear.',body:[
  'An authority gradient is the distance people feel between themselves and someone with more status, confidence, age, expertise or official responsibility. A steep gradient can make people hesitate to ask questions. This matters in community work because small uncertainties, such as who should supervise a doorway or whether a child can leave with someone, can become serious when nobody feels able to speak.',
  'Respectful challenge is not rebellion. It is a disciplined way of protecting the work. Start by assuming good intent, then name the practical concern: what you understood, what seems unclear and what help you need. A calm question gives the other person useful information. It also avoids turning the moment into a contest about pride.',
  'Different kinds of authority should not be blended without thought. Strategic leadership, specialist safeguarding advice and activity coordination are separate responsibilities. One person may hold more than one, but that should be explicit. Long service or a confident tone does not make every instruction appropriate for every situation.',
  'When a visitor asks for a specialist answer, acknowledge the question and connect them to the person responsible for that area. When an instruction changes your agreed duty, check who has authority to change the cover. When a request appears unsafe, pause the activity if needed and seek suitable help.'
 ],example:'“I want to do this properly. I was assigned to the entrance, so who has agreed the cover if I move to the hall?”',reflect:'What phrase could you use to challenge a decision without attacking the person who made it?'},
 {title:'Coordinate before changing duties',time:'5 min',intro:'A helpful move can leave a hidden gap. Make the change visible before you leave your post.',body:[
  'Teams often run into trouble because people respond to the loudest need rather than the whole system. This is understandable. When food service is busy or a queue is growing, the pressure is visible. The unattended entrance, missed handover or unrecorded concern may be less visible until later.',
  'A systems view asks how one action affects the rest of the activity. Before changing duties, use three checks: what needs to change, who will cover the existing task and who needs to know? This turns a personal favour into a coordinated decision. It also protects the person asking for help because the wider impact is no longer hidden.',
  'Good coordination is especially important when people are tired or the environment is busy. Under pressure, humans tend to narrow their attention to the immediate problem. Naming the current duty out loud helps everyone widen the view again: “I can help once we agree entrance cover.”',
  'Urgent safety is different. If someone is in immediate danger, seek emergency or designated help first. Routine cover arrangements should never delay urgent action, and pressure should never push someone into a task they are not trained or able to do.'
 ],example:'“I’m on entrance duty. I can help with registration once someone has taken over here. Can we agree cover first?”',reflect:'Who might be affected if a visible problem is solved by creating a less visible gap elsewhere?'},
 {title:'When instructions conflict',time:'5 min',intro:'Two confident instructions can contradict each other. Your job is to clarify the plan, not decide who deserves more respect.',body:[
  'Conflicting instructions create a social problem as well as a practical one. People may worry about embarrassing someone, appearing difficult or choosing the wrong side. That social pressure can lead to unsafe workarounds: switching back and forth, quietly choosing a favourite instruction or arguing in front of visitors.',
  'The safest response is to describe the contradiction neutrally. Neutral language separates the problem from personal blame. Instead of “you two cannot agree,” say, “I have been asked to use two different entrances. Which route is agreed for this activity?” The words matter because they keep attention on the decision the team needs.',
  'Once a decision is made, repeat it back and make sure the people affected hear the same plan. A clarification that only one person hears can leave confusion in place. If there is no agreed decision maker, ask the organisers to identify one rather than treating confidence, seniority or friendship as the deciding factor.',
  'While waiting for a decision, maintain the last agreed safe arrangement where appropriate. If that arrangement appears unsafe, pause and seek help. Clarity is not only about efficiency; it is part of protecting visitors, volunteers and the organisation.'
 ],example:'“I’ve heard two different plans for the entrance. Let’s check the agreed route before we direct anyone else.”',reflect:'Practise one sentence that names conflicting instructions without blaming either person.'},
 {title:'Feedback, dignity and learning',time:'6 min',intro:'Useful feedback protects dignity while making behaviour clearer. It helps people improve without humiliating them.',body:[
  'Feedback works best when it describes behaviour, impact and next action. Global labels such as careless, rude or useless are hard to act on and easy to experience as an attack. Specific feedback gives the other person a chance to understand what happened and change the next moment.',
  'This connects to attribution bias. Under stress, people often explain another person’s mistake as a character flaw while explaining their own mistake as the result of pressure or unclear information. A good feedback habit slows that bias down. It asks, “What happened, what was the effect and what needs to happen now?” before deciding what the mistake means.',
  'If someone criticises you in front of visitors over a non-urgent issue, keep your immediate response brief and calm. Later, away from the public area, describe the impact and ask for a practical change. You do not have to accept humiliating language as the price of helping.',
  'When giving feedback, be timely, kind and concrete. “Three visitors were unsure where to go; could we make the directions clearer?” is more useful than “you never pay attention.” Repeated intimidation, discrimination or safeguarding concerns should be raised through the appropriate route.'
 ],example:'“I want to understand the correction, but this is not the right place. Can we discuss it quietly once the visitors are settled?”',reflect:'How could you turn “You never help properly” into specific, useful feedback?'},
 {title:'Psychological safety and speaking up',time:'7 min',intro:'People speak up when they believe questions and concerns will be treated seriously rather than punished or mocked.',body:[
  'Psychological safety means people can ask for help, admit uncertainty, challenge a plan and report a concern without fear of humiliation. It does not mean avoiding standards or accepting poor behaviour. It means the team can discuss risks early enough to act on them.',
  'In practical settings, silence is often ambiguous. A quiet person may agree, but they may also be unsure, intimidated, overloaded or worried about consequences. Leaders and experienced volunteers can reduce this uncertainty by inviting concerns directly and responding well when someone raises one.',
  'A useful speaking-up pattern is: name the concern, explain the possible impact and ask for the next step. For example, “I am concerned that we have one adult alone with the children at the side room. What is the approved supervision plan?” This gives the team a route to action without requiring the volunteer to solve everything alone.',
  'Concerns can involve anyone, including a leader. Do not investigate a serious concern yourself, promise secrecy or report only to the person involved. Organisations need an alternative route for concerns about the usual contact, and urgent danger should be escalated immediately.'
 ],example:'“I may be missing something, but I’m concerned the side room is unsupervised. Who should we contact now?”',reflect:'What would make it easier for a new volunteer to raise a concern in your organisation?'},
 {title:'Handovers, records and closing the loop',time:'6 min',intro:'A good handover keeps care consistent. A good record makes responsibility visible after the moment has passed.',body:[
  'Memory is fragile during busy activities. People overestimate what others know and underestimate how quickly details fade. A handover protects the next person from having to reconstruct the situation from hints, assumptions and half-finished tasks.',
  'Use a simple handover structure: what has been done, what is happening now, what still needs attention and what information is relevant. Check that the other person has understood and accepted the handover. A message left somewhere is not the same as a person taking responsibility.',
  'Records should separate observation from interpretation. “At 19:20, Alex said they felt unsafe walking home alone” is more useful than “Alex was being dramatic.” Factual notes help the right person decide what support or safeguarding action is needed. Share only with people who need the information for the task, safety or organisational procedure.',
  'Completion of this training is one step. An organiser still needs to confirm actual duties, support, contacts and readiness before someone begins a role. The purpose of learning is not to create unchecked authority; it is to make safer judgement more likely.'
 ],example:'“The welcome table is ready. Two visitors are waiting for directions. I have recorded one accessibility request and need you to confirm you have taken over.”',reflect:'What three details would you want before accepting responsibility for a duty someone else started?'}
];

