const childrenLessons=[
 {title:'Child development and realistic expectations',time:'6 min',intro:'Children are not small adults. Their thinking, language, impulse control and emotional regulation are still developing.',body:[
  'Development happens in uneven stages. A child may speak confidently but still struggle to wait, plan or understand a long explanation. Another child may be older but need more processing time because of stress, disability, language or unfamiliar surroundings. Age gives clues, but it never tells the whole story.',
  'Executive functions are the mental skills that help people hold information in mind, control impulses and switch attention. These skills develop gradually and are easily disrupted by tiredness, hunger, fear or sensory overload. A child who “knows the rule” may still need adult support to follow it when the room is busy.',
  'Good support adjusts the environment and the instruction. Use short sentences, one step at a time and concrete words. Show rather than only tell. Check understanding by watching what the child does next, not by demanding that they repeat your words under pressure.',
  'Avoid labels such as difficult, dramatic or mature for their age. Labels can make adults stop observing. Describe what you see, check immediate needs and ask the activity lead about agreed adjustments.'
 ],example:'“First put your cup on the tray. Then come back and choose a game with me.”',reflect:'How could you make a two-step instruction easier for a child who looks overwhelmed?'},
 {title:'Serve and return: responsive relationships',time:'6 min',intro:'Children learn safety, language and social confidence through responsive back-and-forth interaction.',body:[
  'Serve and return is a phrase used in child development to describe responsive exchanges between a child and a caring adult. The child “serves” by looking, pointing, asking, crying, showing or moving toward something. The adult “returns” by noticing, naming and responding in a way that fits the child’s signal.',
  'This does not require a special programme. It can happen when an adult notices a child looking uncertain and says, “You are looking for the craft table; I can show you.” The important part is responsiveness. The child experiences that communication has an effect and that adults can be predictable.',
  'Serve and return also supports language and emotional regulation. Naming what is happening helps children connect words with feelings and actions. Leaving space for an answer tells the child that their response matters. Ignoring or rushing past repeated bids for connection can increase distress or withdrawal.',
  'In a community setting, volunteers are not replacing parents, carers or professionals. They are creating small moments of reliable attention inside the activity’s boundaries.'
 ],example:'“You pointed to the quieter table. Would you like to sit there first and watch for a minute?”',reflect:'What child “serves” might adults miss when a room is busy?'},
 {title:'Co-regulation before correction',time:'7 min',intro:'Children borrow calm from regulated adults before they can reliably calm themselves.',body:[
  'Co-regulation means an adult helps a child manage feelings and behaviour through calm presence, predictable words and safe limits. It is not letting the child do anything they want. It is guiding behaviour in a state where the child can hear and use the guidance.',
  'When a child is upset, the reasoning part of the brain may not be leading the moment. Long lectures, public shaming or rapid instructions can add more load. Start by regulating yourself: lower your voice, slow your pace, soften your posture and keep a respectful distance.',
  'Name the feeling without claiming certainty, then state the safe behaviour clearly. “You look frustrated. Hands stay safe.” This gives the child words and a boundary. Then offer a limited choice that keeps the adult responsible for safety: “You can sit by the window or help me carry the cards.”',
  'Specific praise after recovery helps learning stick. “You moved your hands away and took a breath” teaches more than “good job” because it names the behaviour the child can repeat.'
 ],example:'“It is okay to feel angry. Hands stay safe. You can stand beside me or sit at the quiet table.”',reflect:'Turn “stop being disruptive” into one calm, specific direction.'},
 {title:'Behaviour as communication',time:'6 min',intro:'Behaviour often communicates a need, skill gap or feeling before a child can explain it in words.',body:[
  'Seeing behaviour as communication does not mean excusing harm. It means asking what the behaviour may be telling adults so the response fits the need. A child running around may be seeking movement, avoiding embarrassment, reacting to noise or testing whether adults are predictable.',
  'Use the ABC lens: what happened before the behaviour, what the behaviour looked like and what happened after. This helps adults notice patterns rather than relying on blame. If a child repeatedly leaves a group when reading aloud begins, the trigger may be anxiety or difficulty with the task rather than deliberate disrespect.',
  'Respond to safety first, then teach the replacement behaviour. A replacement behaviour is the acceptable action that meets the same need. “Ask for a break card” is more useful than “do not run away” because it gives the child a way to succeed.',
  'Share patterns with the authorised activity lead rather than creating your own behaviour plan. Consistency matters, and some behaviour may signal safeguarding, health or additional support needs.'
 ],example:'“When the music starts you cover your ears and move away. Would headphones or a quieter job help?”',reflect:'What need might sit behind a behaviour that adults usually describe as attention-seeking?'},
 {title:'Boundaries in person and online',time:'6 min',intro:'Clear boundaries protect children, volunteers and the trustworthiness of the organisation.',body:[
  'Boundaries are not coldness. They are the agreed edges that make warmth safer. Children should know which adults are supervising them, where activities happen, how messages are sent, what physical contact is appropriate and what to do if they feel uncomfortable.',
  'Know the organisation’s supervision, photography, transport, physical-contact, toileting, messaging and one-to-one rules before the activity. Do not move a conversation to a personal account, arrange private contact or create exceptions because a child seems to trust you.',
  'Ask before ordinary physical help where the child can choose. Some urgent safety actions cannot wait, but explain what you are doing as soon as possible and report the event through the agreed route. Never ask a child to keep your interaction secret. Surprises have an ending; secrecy can isolate a child.',
  'Boundary concerns should be recorded factually and reported. A volunteer who feels unsure should ask early rather than improvising alone.'
 ],example:'“I cannot message from my personal account. Let’s use the activity’s approved channel so the right adults can see it.”',reflect:'Which local policies must you know before taking or sharing a photograph?'},
 {title:'Listening to concerns and disclosures',time:'7 min',intro:'A calm first response can help a child keep talking and reach the right support.',body:[
  'When a child shares a worry or possible harm, the adult’s first job is to listen, not investigate. Shock, disbelief, anger or a rush of questions can make the child retreat or change their words to please the adult. A steady response communicates that the child has done the right thing by telling.',
  'Use open, minimal prompts such as “tell me what happened” or “what happened next?” Avoid leading questions that suggest an answer. Do not promise secrecy. Explain in simple language that you need to share the information with someone whose role is to help keep them safe.',
  'Write down what the child said as soon as possible, using their own words where you can. Record time, date, context and observations. Keep interpretation separate from facts. Do not confront the person named or try to confirm the story yourself.',
  'Follow the organisation’s safeguarding procedure promptly. If there is immediate danger, seek emergency help. The child should not be left carrying the risk because adults are unsure which internal route to use.'
 ],example:'“Thank you for telling me. I am taking you seriously. I cannot keep this secret, but I will share it with someone whose job is to help keep you safe.”',reflect:'What could you say instead of promising to keep a disclosure secret?'},
 {title:'Inclusion, adaptation and handover',time:'6 min',intro:'Inclusion means removing barriers while keeping the same respect, safety and meaningful expectations.',body:[
  'Inclusive support begins with curiosity. Ask what helps rather than assuming from a diagnosis, disability, language, age or behaviour. Barriers may sit in the environment: noise, lighting, written instructions, pace, crowded spaces, unfamiliar routines or a lack of choice.',
  'Adapt the route without lowering dignity. A child might watch first, use a visual prompt, answer without speech, take a quieter role or have extra time to move between activities. The goal is participation with safety and respect, not making the child stand out unnecessarily.',
  'Privacy still matters. Share only relevant information with people who need it to support the child. Do not discuss a child’s personal situation in public areas or with volunteers who are simply curious.',
  'At handover, tell the authorised person what has helped, what remains difficult and any concern that needs follow-up. Confirm they have understood and accepted responsibility.'
 ],example:'“Would it help to see the steps on a card, or would you like me to show you once before you try?”',reflect:'What is one barrier you could change without changing the aim of an activity?'}
];

