const olderLessons=[
 {title:'Dignity, autonomy and ageism',time:'6 min',intro:'Support starts with the person’s preferences and abilities, not assumptions about age, disability or vulnerability.',body:[
  'Ageism means stereotypes, prejudice or discrimination based on age. It can appear in obvious insults, but it also appears in well-meant habits: speaking over someone, using a childlike tone, assuming memory problems, ignoring a person’s preference or treating an older adult as a task rather than a decision-maker.',
  'Vulnerability should not erase autonomy. A person may need help with mobility but make every other decision clearly. Another person may need support to understand a form but still know what they prefer. Good support asks, explains and gives time.',
  'Use the person’s preferred name and speak directly to them. If a relative, carer or professional is present, include them appropriately without making them the only audience. Describe support needs precisely rather than using labels such as helpless, confused or difficult.',
  'Autonomy also includes the right to decline help unless there is an immediate safety concern or a lawful process says otherwise. Volunteers should not make legal determinations alone; they should follow organisational procedure and involve the authorised person.'
 ],example:'“Would you like me to bring the chair closer, or would you prefer to walk over together?”',reflect:'Where might a well-meant offer of help accidentally remove someone’s choice?'},
 {title:'Communication barriers and accessible pace',time:'6 min',intro:'Attention, environment and pace often matter as much as the words you choose.',body:[
  'Communication problems are not always located inside the person. A noisy room, poor lighting, fast speech, small print, unfamiliar vocabulary or too many choices can create barriers. The first step is to make the situation easier before assuming the person cannot understand.',
  'Face the person, make sure your face is visible, reduce background noise where possible and check whether they use glasses, hearing aids, a communication board or another support. Speak clearly at a natural pace. Shouting or exaggerating speech can feel patronising and may make lip-reading harder.',
  'Use one idea at a time and allow pauses. If a sentence is not understood, rephrase it rather than repeating the same words more loudly. Ask before finishing someone’s sentence. Some people need time to retrieve words, and rushing can increase frustration.',
  'When choices feel tiring, offer two clear options while leaving room for another preference. Accessible communication is not about simplifying the person; it is about reducing unnecessary effort.'
 ],example:'“Would you like tea or water? We can choose something else too.”',reflect:'How could you change a busy room before assuming someone cannot understand?'},
 {title:'Distress, confusion and unmet needs',time:'7 min',intro:'A changed behaviour may signal pain, fear, sensory overload, fatigue, trauma or another unmet need.',body:[
  'Distress is information. A person who repeatedly asks to leave, refuses help or becomes agitated may be communicating discomfort, confusion, pain, fear, embarrassment or a need for control. Dismissing distress as “just old age” can delay practical help.',
  'Start with observation: what changed, when did it change and what was happening nearby? Consider noise, temperature, hunger, medication timing, toileting needs, pain, loneliness, past experiences and whether the person understands what is happening now.',
  'If someone appears confused, repeated correction can sometimes increase distress. Acknowledge the feeling even if the facts seem mistaken, then gently orient only if it helps. “You seem worried about getting home. You are safe here with me while I contact the activity lead” responds to the need without humiliating the person.',
  'Volunteers should not diagnose dementia, mental illness or capacity. They can notice changes, reduce pressure, seek authorised help and record facts. If you feel yourself becoming frustrated, pause or ask a colleague to take over when safe.'
 ],example:'“You seem worried about getting home. You are safe here with me. Shall we sit somewhere quieter while I contact the activity lead?”',reflect:'List three possible needs that could sit behind someone repeatedly asking to leave.'},
 {title:'Consent, capacity and everyday choice',time:'6 min',intro:'Do not treat vulnerability as permission. Consent is specific, ongoing and connected to the actual decision being made.',body:[
  'Consent means the person agrees to a particular action after being given enough information in a way they can understand. It is not a one-off mood or a blanket permission. Someone may consent to being guided to a seat and decline help with a coat a minute later.',
  'Capacity is decision-specific. A person might be able to choose lunch but not understand a complex financial document. Volunteers should not decide legal capacity alone. If capacity is in question, involve the authorised lead and follow the applicable policy and law.',
  'Support decision-making by slowing down, using plain words, checking understanding and reducing unnecessary choices. Do not pressure someone by saying everyone else has agreed or that they are making things difficult.',
  'If a person refuses help and there is no immediate danger, respect the refusal and seek advice if you are worried. If there is immediate danger, follow emergency or safeguarding procedures.'
 ],example:'“I can explain the two options again, or we can pause and ask the activity lead to help us work through it.”',reflect:'How can you support a choice without taking the decision away from the person?'},
 {title:'Financial, personal and digital boundaries',time:'6 min',intro:'Kindness can become unsafe when volunteers cross personal, financial or digital boundaries.',body:[
  'People may ask for help with money, forms, transport, medicine, messages or private arrangements because they trust you. Trust is valuable, but it does not remove the need for boundaries. Financial and personal matters can create risk for the person and for the volunteer.',
  'Do not borrow money, accept unusual gifts, handle bank cards, store PINs, become a beneficiary, manage medication or use private contact channels outside policy. Even when intentions are kind, these actions can create dependence, suspicion, exploitation or allegations that are difficult to resolve.',
  'Digital boundaries matter too. Personal messaging can hide interactions from the organisation and make support unavailable if you are absent. Use approved channels and record relevant contact according to policy.',
  'When you cannot help directly, respond with respect and connect the person to the approved process. A boundary should not sound like abandonment; it should point to safer support.'
 ],example:'“I cannot help with your bank card, but I can contact the agreed person who supports payment questions.”',reflect:'What could make an apparently kind financial favour unsafe for both people?'},
 {title:'Recognising safeguarding concerns',time:'7 min',intro:'Small observations can matter. One sign does not prove harm, but it may need reporting.',body:[
  'Adult safeguarding concerns can include abuse, neglect, coercion, exploitation, degrading treatment, unexplained injuries, sudden financial changes, fearfulness, isolation or a marked change in behaviour. The volunteer’s job is not to prove what happened. It is to notice, record and raise concerns through the right route.',
  'Listen without leading questions. If the person shares a concern, take them seriously and do not promise secrecy. Explain that you may need to share the information with someone whose role is to help keep them safe.',
  'Avoid confronting a suspected person or investigating alone. This can increase risk, contaminate information and put the volunteer outside their role. If immediate danger or a medical emergency is present, contact emergency services.',
  'Concerns may involve a leader, relative, carer or another volunteer. Organisations need an alternative reporting route for those situations. Ask for it before you need it.'
 ],example:'“I noticed the bruise today at 14:10. Sam said, ‘I don’t want him to come back.’ I will record those words and contact the safeguarding lead.”',reflect:'What is the difference between noticing possible signs and proving abuse?'},
 {title:'Records, handovers and continuity of support',time:'6 min',intro:'Good records and handovers help the next person provide support without guessing.',body:[
  'Continuity matters because people should not have to repeat sensitive information to every new volunteer. At the same time, personal information should not spread further than necessary. The skill is to share enough with the right person and no more.',
  'A good record separates facts from interpretation. Include what you saw, what you heard, who was present, date, time and what action you took. Avoid loaded language. “Pat declined tea twice and held their stomach” is more useful than “Pat was being awkward.”',
  'At handover, tell the authorised person what support has been agreed, what concerns remain and what needs follow-up. Confirm they have accepted responsibility. If a concern is urgent, do not leave it buried in a routine note.',
  'Records are not a substitute for human care. They support memory, accountability and decision-making, but the person should still be treated with warmth, patience and privacy.'
 ],example:'“Pat preferred the quiet corner, declined food, and said their stomach hurt. I have told the activity lead and recorded the time.”',reflect:'How do observation and interpretation differ in a safeguarding record?'}
];

